Or maybe there's a simpler model: register with email, password, and tenant_id\n\nSince there's no tenant management endpoint, I'll assume:\n- Register takes email, password, and tenant_id (required)\n- The first user in a tenant is an admin\n- Subsequent users are viewers by default\n\nLet me think about the event structure more carefully:\n- name: string\n- properties: JSON object (stored as string in SQLite)\n- timestamp: ISO datetime string (defaults to now)\n- user_id: set from authenticated user\n- session_id: optional\n- tenant_id: set from authenticated user's tenant\n\nFor the query engine:\n- Count: count events matching name filter, with optional time range\n- Timeseries: group by time buckets (hour, day, week, month)\n- Breakdown: group by a property value, extracted from the JSON properties field\n\nFor the export, I need to use SQLite JSON functions to extract property values.\n\nLet me also think about what \"properties\" looks like in events. The schema says `properties TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'`. So properties are stored as JSON strings. To query by property, I'll use `json_extract(properties, '$.key')` in SQLite.\n\nNow let me start building. I'll write the contract first, then implement everything.\n\nLet me think carefully about the complete implementation.\n\nFor the password hashing, I'll use `crypto.pbkdf2Sync` or `crypto.scryptSync`. Let me use scrypt since it's more modern.\n\nFor the Express app, let me use JSON middleware and a structured approach.\n\nLet me now write all files. I'll organize my implementation carefully.\n\nLet me think about the structure more carefully:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  index.ts         - Entry point, exports createApp\n  app.ts           - Creates Express app with routes\n  db.ts            - Database class, initializes schema\n  middleware/\n    auth.ts        - JWT authentication middleware  \n    errorHandler.ts - Global error handler\n  routes/\n    auth.ts        - Registration, login, profile endpoints\n    users.